I am fanboy hear me roar so I don't have  any particular impetus to talk about  this particular subject but it was on my  mind today and I thought I'd record some  thoughts and the question occurred to me  how much should creators of  entertainment listen to the fans because  we've entered an era of unprecedented  connection between fans of some piece of  entertainment and the makers of that  entertainment thanks to social media  Twitter Instagram whatever we can reach  out if not directly to them at the very  least we can consolidate in a unified  voice through social media that is loud  enough that they will hear it  even if we can't do a direct one-on-one  communication with them but is it really  a good thing  and what I say creators I don't even  mean exclusively the original creators  because we live in the age of adaptation  so oftentimes I'm also referring to the  people who are adapting an existing  piece of media because they certainly  have their you have their ears on what  the fans are saying as well I don't want  to say you have to listen to what they  want and I'll go into some of the  reasons why maybe you probably shouldn't  but you shouldn't deliberately go out of  your way to tick them off I think really  you want to go way back historic the big  case in point Arthur Conan Doyle was  really really sick of writing Sherlock  so let me kill him until the fan out  where I was so loud and nothing else he  was doing was bringing much of a  paycheck he was just he just went fine  he helped bring them back I mean the  only reason he killed Sherlock wasn't  because like he had been building to it  it wasn't his master plan it was it it  wasn't you know some part of conclusion  of a grand stories like he just was sick  of writing it and so he did indeed just  kill off like bear stop asking me to  write more here's the problem with fans  and but listening to fans ask a fan what  they want and what they'll tell you is  they want more of the stuff they already  love and that is how you get in  basically into in this  you know masturbatory nostalgia where  we're just cycling around the same stuff  over and over and over again because you  ask us what we want and we tell you it's  it's what we already love most fans  aren't gonna think of the new thing that  they don't even know that they will love  once you give it to them  so Wow fan reaction to something that  you have done is probably valuable and  worth you know getting a read on what is  a bad idea is sort of preemptively  trying to get a gauge on what fans will  think before you've actually done  something and also fans we need to  understand our place in all this as well  because when we're talking about  something like a comic book a book  series yes the fans keep it going but  also in a lot of those cases there are  fewer creators involved in it makes it  easier for them to sort of maintain  their level of control like I don't  think george RR martin gives a dang what  any fan thinks about anything that he's  writing for these last two books because  it's his is 100% his and he does not  behold into anybody else  the more people get involved the more  voices are like maybe did not do that  and you know again you can get the  stagnation thing but as things get into  bigger bigger mediums like television  like film especially we need to  understand as fans that we are not  enough on our own to make something  profitable especially in the case of a  movie now that doesn't mean that our  opinions don't matter or shouldn't count  but we need to understand that when you  look at the box office for the latest  superhero blockbuster the percentage of  that box office that is the fans I mean  like the fans who were fans of this  thing before it was even a movie that's  a small percentage of the overall box  office they need the mainstream who  maybe folks who have heard of whatever  the thing is but they're not devoted  fans of it and without  the thing doesn't become a glorified hit  now that doesn't mean fans are  irrelevant because sometimes you know if  fans are loud enough about this is awful  that can filter out to the mainstream  they go oh well the people who like this  thing don't even like this movie I'm not  gonna bother with that so again it can  be a loud and a powerful voice but raw  numbers we don't have as much power as  we think we do and yet especially in the  case of movies and and television but  movies especially studios are terrified  of fans which has led to sort of my big  bug bear with adaptations lately which  is a fear of actually properly adapting  a material because at this point I think  studios are so a desperate to meet fans  approval and so debt and so intensely  terrified about them crapping all over  something and potentially poisoning the  minds of the mainstream audience when  they do so that when especially a book  gets adapted into a film or a TV show  they change almost nothing they at best  they'll remove a few things to get the  runtime down to a reasonable length but  they will change almost nothing and I'm  sure fans will go why isn't that the  best thing ever why would we want it  changed well two reasons first of all  different things work better in  different media you need to be you need  to allow for certain embellishments  especially in the case of film if you've  got a filmmaker of any degree of vision  you need to allow them to put their own  stamp on it otherwise you're just gonna  get completely cookie cutter your  adaptations of stuff I like to use the  Harry Potter movies as a good example on  this so the first two Harry Potter  movies are by far the blandest of the  lot and they're also the most faithful  in terms of what they adapt to the  screen they keep the most stuff they  they add the least in terms of you know  things moments or ideas or character  beats that that didn't originate in the  novels but still make sense starting  with the third one and going forward  that stuff started having more and more  and that in my mind is proper adaptation  just doing a copy/paste job from a novel  to a script and then just filming that  and going out a checklist of all the  scenes the fans are expecting to see  that's not proper adaptation in my book  that's that's just a really expensive  fan film proper adaptation requires  tweaking the material so that it works  better in the medium you're now telling  the story in this is a big part of why I  really have a strong distaste for the  Watchmen movie I know some people like  it but I actually hate for how slavishly  faithful it is because that story was  written so specifically to work on the  comic book page and they didn't make the  adjustments you need to to make it work  better on film and so the film is just  an inferior version of the same story  because it's not being told in the  medium it was designed for now this is  actually part of why I have really  enjoyed a lot of the superhero  adaptations that have happened because  very few of them have been adapting  specific storylines directly they've  been pulling from all sorts of  influences but by and large these films  from whatever studio's making them it's  been true of the DC EU movies has been  drove the MCU these films are taking a  superhero character and even if they're  retelling like the origin which is a  specific story they're just hitting a  couple of beats about it but then  allowing all these things from all these  years to seep into it all these  characters across a hero's entire  timeline you know bringing in villains  that probably weren't even around what  the character truly had their origins  story and all this other stuff and that  is proper adaptation that rather than  trying to go rigidly alright this story  here as a movie they are going okay  here's the character here's the essence  of this character here's the essence of  their villains here's the essence of the  kinds of stories that they're in how  we get something that captures all of  this and that's why something like say  Civil War was so good you know it took  its title from a very specific  storyline from the Marvel Comics and  borrowed a couple of key elements from  it it is a different there's almost no  similarity plot wise in terms of the  Civil War comic book and the Civil War  movie they bear a very superficial  relationship to each other and in my  mind that's perfect  because I can recognize the elements  where they took the story points but  they've done something new with it now  granted all of that is easier when you  are doing comic book you know superhero  stories specifically that with comic  books that have been ongoing for a very  long time and aren't defined by this is  the definitive story it gets harder when  you're adapting a specific story which  if you're doing something other than  superheroes that's largely what you're  doing you're adapting a specific story  but do you want to know what kind of  movies you get when you allow a  filmmaker to take the core ideas and the  core characters from a source work but  do their own thing completely with it  you get your asset park you get jaws if  you I mean odds are you've probably seen  either or both of those movies and I  didn't mean to pick two Steven Spielberg  movies there's they both happen to be  really good examples of this but in both  cases the books very different and in my  personal opinion the books are not as  good the jaws book is yes kind of a mess  honestly there's a lot of weird and ugly  and messy subplots that the movie just  gets rid of and as far as Jurassic Park  goes the the book the book is a lot more  technically minded and as a result it  kind of loses the threat of just the fun  of look at these dinosaurs running  around cuz it gets kind of hung up on  the science we just don't like Michael  Crichton tended to do now some people do  prefer that but I think whether you  prefer the book or the movie of Jurassic  Park you do get the wonderful experience  that each is a different experience  to have and both are valid and both are  recognizable as being connected to each  other but you don't see the movie and go  well I don't need to read the book now  no you can't sir read the book and get a  completely different experience and yet  still recognize that it is the same  thing but when you remain slavish to the  source material which is what happens  when creators and studios are so scared  of fans as they are right now we just  get boring things I mean especially for  fans and that's something I will never  understand where fans get overly upset  about how much things got changed from  the original works like but you you've  read the book I mean do you really want  something that is nothing but the thing  you already know I mean I get the  nostalgic value in that but aren't you  gonna be bored and look having said that  that not every divergence that ever  happens with an adaptation when they do  go their own direction with it actually  works The Hobbit trilogy which I can't  stand took a lot of you know it went its  own way with it I won't take that away  from it but boy didn't work but under  the but the reason it didn't work is  because it lost the heart of the  material the reason the Hobbit films are  a really poor adaptation of a hobbit  aren't because of the plot points that  got changed is because they changed the  purpose of it because the purpose of the  Hobbit the book is to follow Bilbo on a  fun adventure that he goes on the  purpose of The Hobbit movies is to build  up to Lord of the Rings they're serving  completely different functions and at  their heart they don't have the same  mission they don't have the same intent  and that is what leads to all the other  junk about those movies that doesn't  work but at the same time I have to  recognize that if they had done movies  that were nothing except exactly what  was in the book I would have been bored  I mean it's a big part of why the Lord  of the Rings movies worked is because I  mean the Lord of the Rings books they're  dry Affairs they read almost like  history texts so to  give it that more adventurous feel the  more adventurous vibe to ditch a lot of  the history and the men the political  minutia and the goings-on of that sort  of stuff in people's lineage and all  that to toss that aside and focus on the  action in the combat which is not as  prevalent in the novels that is proper  adaptation because that is the sort of  stuff that film does well so that is  where it was correct by keeping the  heart of the story still intact and it  is the journey to take care of the ring  it is the the bonding and the end the  stress put on the bonds between these  characters and keeping an eye on that  but putting more of a focus on the  action than was in the books because  again the action is a more visceral  thing film does visceral better than it  does the more intellectual expositional  stuff which is all over the novels and  yet I still know fans of the book who  get really upset about specific minutiae  details that didn't make the movie I get  when something that you loved doesn't  make the cut it's disappointing I've had  that happen with stuff that I knew the  source material really well before  seeing the adaptation but I will swallow  that go like would that thing that I  wish was there truly have made this  adaptation a superior piece of work and  nine times out of ten the answer is no  not really it just would have made me  happy so as I said I really think that  creators need to be aware of fan  reaction and fan desires to a certain  extent but also not feel that they are  beholden to them because I think you can  see in the way that certain television  shows evolve especially over time that  oftentimes the longer a TV show goes on  and the more the fans keep calling for  something to happen eventually it does  and then the show's not as good I mean  the classic example is just any show  that had a will they won't they you know  sort of relationship between a male and  female character once they do the show  isn't the same after that and it's  usually some  the fans have been calling for like just  get them together and then you do and  it's like well what's the point now like  you should never you should never do  that unless you're about to end the show  you you should always be dangling those  sorts of things I mean it's the reason  I'm Doctor Who I I'm putting my foot  down  I never want to see a ginger doctor I  want the doctor wishing to be ginger  every single regeneration if they ever  make the doctor ginger that should be  the last regeneration the doctor ever  gets because after that what more if you  have to tease us with I'm being slightly  facetious I that kind of exaggerating a  very minor point but it's still it's it  speaks to my overall personal sentiment  so I think I've rambled on and gone  tangent enough for this particular video  so what are your thoughts on any of this  whatever they are drop something down in  the comments let's talk about it don't  forget to like subscribe check me out on  Twitter right council of geeks give a  listen at the council of geeks podcast  available on iTunes and stitcher I'm  also one half of the punch like a girl  podcast also available on iTunes and  stitcher so until next time this council  is adjourned  [Applause]  [Applause]
 

